imagine you've got millions of database  entries across thousands of bigquery  data sets hosted on hundreds of google  cloud projects and you have to search  through all that data to make sure  sensitive data is properly protected  that sounds like a huge deal and to be  honest it's a lot just thinking about it  luckily though automatic dlp makes it a  breeze you can scan across your org with  just a few clicks automatic dlp offers a  slew of new features to configure scans  that meet your business needs so that  you can have even more visibility into  at-risk data in your organization  in our last video what is automatic dlp  we outlined what automatic dlp is  now we'll learn how to configure it and  make it work for your business  to be able to scan your organization  data and create a data profile you need  to create a scan configuration  a scan configuration is where you set  the resource that you want to scan  organization folder or project  which inspection template to use and  what to do with the results  but before we can dive into how to  create a scan configuration let's go  over some resource hierarchy  google cloud resources are organized  hierarchically starting from the bottom  of the hierarchy projects are the first  level resources like bigquery data sets  are associated with individual projects  folders are an additional grouping  mechanism on top of projects folders can  be used to model different legal  entities departments and teams within a  company  folders and projects are all mapped  under the organization resource the  organization resource is the root node  of the google cloud resource hierarchy  and all resources that belong to an  organization are grouped under the  organization node  this provides central visibility and  control over every resource that belongs  to an organization  automatic dlp is designed to operate  across this hierarchy enabling you to  configure it across projects folders or  your organization this gives you  flexibility while minimizing overhead  let's talk a bit about scanning  organizations and folders a maximum of  one scan configuration can be created  for each organization or folder once the  scan configuration is active it will  cover all the data sets and tables in  all the projects below it in the  hierarchy if two or more active scan  configurations have the same project in  their scope the following rules apply  the one that is closest to the project  in the hierarchy will be able to  generate data profiles for that project  so if you want to cover your entire  organization you can just create one  configuration for your org  if you need to have different  configurations for a certain department  or business unit you have that  flexibility too  now let's talk about project level  configurations automatic dlp treats  project level scan configurations  independently of organization and folder  level configurations  this way you can enable project or data  owners to run their own discovery scans  without impacting what the organization  level needs  so a scan configuration that you create  at the project level can't override one  that you create for a parent folder or  organization  let's take a look at an example where  there are three scan configurations here  scan configuration 1 applies to the  entire organization scan configuration 2  applies to the team b folder and scan  configuration 3 applies to the  production project  in this example cloud dlp effectively  generates two sets of profiles for the  production project  one set for scan configuration 2 and one  set for scan configuration three  however even though there are two sets  of profiles for the same project you  don't see them all together in your  dashboard you only see the profiles that  were generated under the purview and  region that you're currently viewing  and speaking of things under your  purview let's talk about roles and  permissions  to turn a scan configuration on at the  oregon folder level you need the dlp  administrator role the project creator  role and either the organization  administrator or the security admin role  on the organization iam policy  viewing data profiles on an organization  or folder will require the dlp data  profiles reader role and the dlp reader  role  that being said it is possible for a  user to create the configuration another  user with higher permissions to grant  profiling access and another user to  monitor the generated profiles  similarly to enable automatic dlp on at  the project level you need the right  project level permissions  okay now that resource hierarchy and  permissions are covered let's get into  the good stuff  as i mentioned before a scan  configuration is where you set the  resource that you want to scan  which inspection template to use and  what to do with the results  configurations can only be created once  for an org and once for any folder but  you always have the flexibility to  change it  first make sure that you're in the  correct organization or project for this  demonstration i'm going to create a  configuration at the organization level  to do that i've got the security admin  role assigned on the organization iam  policy so that i can configure automatic  dlp for the entire org we'll also touch  on the project level scan a bit later  in manage schedules you can set a  scanning schedule for a subset of your  data with details on filters  frequency and conditions filters specify  the subset of your data and you can  identify specific projects data sets or  table ids and regular expressions that  you want to call out which means you can  leave out or only scan any portion of  the data you want like tables that end  in underscore temp  frequency covers whether selected tables  should be profiled how often they should  be profiled and events that will trigger  scanning for example you can specify how  often you want the data reprofiled when  the schema changes like when columns are  removed or when a table has data changes  such as when new data rows are added  finally conditions are set up so that  they must be met before a table is  profiled there are minimum conditions  which are about rows and duration  and time condition for only profiling  tables after a specific date and time  so if you have tables that are created  but not filled until 48 hours later  and you don't want them profiled while  they're empty you consider row condition  though the table must have at least 100  rows before being profiled or must be 48  hours old  managing schedules is considered an  optional step because if you don't set a  new schedule the default schedule will  take over  the default frequency is to pick up new  tables daily and only reprofile monthly  if the schema changes  next you'll need to select an inspection  template to specify the types of  sensitive data to scan for  for today we'll create a new one and  we'll scan for dates of birth  emails and person names  you can also add additional inspection  rule sets if you want to exclude  unwanted findings or include additional  findings  then we'll select a likelihood level if  possible and press continue to finish  our inspection template  keep in mind that if you change an  inspection template that your scan  configuration uses the changes are  applied  only to future scans and any existing  data profiles are not overwritten  so if you edit your template to add an  info type that change only affects  tables that have yet to be scanned  now you get to decide how to manage the  outcome of your scan aka those data  profiles  you have the option to publish to  chronicle  save copies to bigquery  or publish events to pub sub but we'll  talk more about this in an upcoming  video  up next is the service agent container  which is a project that cloud dlp uses  to track billing charges related to  organization and folder level profiling  a service agent is a google managed  service account that automatic dlp uses  to profile data on your behalf  when you create a scan configuration for  your organization or for a folder cloud  dlp requires you to provide a service  agent to authenticate your service agent  must have all the required permissions  to access and profile your data  cloud dlp can automatically create a  service agent for you and if you're  configuring project level profiling  cloud dlp automatically uses your  project's own service agent  for this demonstration we'll create a  new project  last but not least you need to set a  location to store the configuration this  is just the region for where the  configuration will live remember that  regardless of where you store your  configuration when scanning your actual  data automatic dlp will do so in the  same geographic region that your data  lives in this way your data does not  leave its region and data residency is  supported  we're going to go with multiple regions  in the united states for our resource  location  then we'll review this quickly and  create  now you have your scan configuration  saved in your dashboard and it will run  on its own  before we finish let's talk about the  project level configuration i mentioned  before  as we discussed this type of scan  configuration can always profile the  target project and does not compete with  other configurations at the level of the  parent folder or organization  the main difference is that there's no  need to select a service agent container  everything else is the same as with the  organization and folders  now you've seen firsthand how simple it  is to create and deeply customize a scan  configuration for your organization  folder or project  so let's recap  scan configurations is where you set the  resource that you want to scan which  inspection template to use and what to  do with the results  google cloud resources are organized  hierarchically and automatic dlp scan  configurations follow this hierarchy  when scanning  the correct permissions are needed when  turning scan configurations on  scan configurations now include new  options for your org like how often to  scan what to scan or not scan  where to scan and more  then you just turn it on once and let it  run while we take care of the rest join  us next time when we explore how to  manage your scan outcomes in detail  thanks for watching  you
 

